**Why AGPL rather than GPL**: Qianmo's second product line is a communication
and collaboration network connecting resident agents. The most likely way for
it to be taken without reciprocity is for someone to run it as a hosted
service -- which GPL-3.0 does not reach and AGPL-3.0 section 13 (Remote
Network Interaction) does. For a network-shaped product, only AGPL is strict
in substance.

**The MIT-to-AGPL direction is permitted**: MIT is permissive and may be
incorporated into an AGPL work; the reverse is not true. Contributing this
repository's changes to the base layer back upstream (MIT) therefore requires
a separate grant. The base project and this repository share one owner, so
dual licensing is available, but it is **not automatic** and is decided case
by case. Per roadmap v2.41 the current position on upstreaming is "not for
now, revisit individually".

**This change is not retroactive**: the repository was distributed under MIT
before this change. Copies obtained under MIT keep that grant; AGPL applies
only to versions published after this change.

**Section 13 binds us too**: AGPL-3.0 section 13 requires an operator to offer
Corresponding Source to users who interact with the program over a network.
Qianmo's console and its resident nodes are exactly that, **including the
deployments we run ourselves**. This is the one ongoing obligation the
relicensing adds, and it is not a clause that only applies to other people.

